<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yadavindra Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yadavindra Singh was the last ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Patiala, reigning from 1938, and served as Chancellor of the Chamber of Princes. He acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947 and in 1948 became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a>, the constitutional head, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a>, which was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1956. He later served as an Indian ambassador and was a noted cricketer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yadavindra-singh.jpg" alt="Yadavindra Singh" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob Mieremet for Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yadvinder_Singh_Mahendra_Bahadur_(1971).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yadavindra Singh, also spelt Yadvinder Singh, was born in Patiala City into a Jat Sikh family of the Sidhu clan and attended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aitchison-college">Aitchison College</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>. He served in the Patiala State Police, rising to Inspector General, and played one Test for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1934. In 1935 he married Hem Prabha Devi of Saraikela State. He succeeded his father, Bhupinder Singh, on 23 March 1938 and the same year married Mehtab Kaur; the stated reason was that his first wife had no children, though it was speculated that Akali leaders wanted the heir of Patiala born to a Sikh mother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He was chancellor of the Chamber of Princes from 1943 to 1944 and its pro-chancellor in 1947, when at a special session he urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> to unite with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and persuaded many rulers to join. He served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and from 1938 to 1960 he was president of the Indian Olympic Association, leading the founding and organisation of the first Asian Games in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1951.</p><p>During the partition of 1947 numerous pogroms took place in and around Patiala, in several cases carried out by organised bands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>. Harkishan Singh Surjeet of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> (Marxist) later claimed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> was involved and took part in the massacres, Nehru believed he had sought to drive the Muslims out of the territory, and Patiala's foreign minister, Bari Ram Sharma, denied that any Patiala soldier had been involved in killings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He kept his royal titles until 1971, when the 26th Amendment to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a> abolished princely titles. In 1956 he gave the Anand Bhawan palace to the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and Moti Bagh Palace to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a>, which made it the Netaji Subhas National Institute of Sports; he also founded Yadavindra Public School, housed in the Lal Bagh Palace he donated. A keen horticulturist, he chaired the Indian Horticulture Development Council.</p><p>He was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s delegate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> in 1956 and 1957 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1958, and led its delegation to the Food and Agriculture Organization at times between 1959 and 1969. After serving as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1965 to 1966, he became ambassador to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1971 and died suddenly of heart failure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 17 June 1974. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a>'s instructions he was cremated with full state honours. His son Amarinder Singh became Chief Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yadavindra Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1948 eight princely states, Patiala State, Nabha State, Jind State, Kapurthala State, Faridkot State, Malerkotla, Nalagarh and Kalsia State, were merged into the Patiala and East Punjab States Union, with Maharaja <b>Yadavindra Singh</b> of Patiala as Rajpramukh.</blockquote><a href="/state/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Yadavindra Singh</b> acceded to India in 1947.</blockquote><a href="/state/patiala-state">Patiala State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Patiala, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.57,76.63&amp;year=1952" aria-label="Patiala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6536 1049 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yadavindra Singh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6986 1319h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6986 1319h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.57,76.63&amp;year=1952">What was here in 1952? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
